# A KNOWN GAP, pinned so it cannot become folklore.
#
# `define_method` is extracted only when its name is an interpolated literal
# inside a binding the extractor can read (`[:one, :two].each { define_method
# "#{n}_step" }`). A plain symbol — `define_method(:plain)` — falls through and
# yields no unit at all. That is inherited from trekr's extractor, not
# introduced here: trekr answers the same way on the same input.
#
# Two consequences, in increasing order of surprise:
#   1. `Alpha#plain` and `Gadget#save` are invisible to `--symbols` and to the
#      index. Not merely unsummarizable — absent.
#   2. `Gadget#save` therefore cannot be reported as a clone of `Widget#save`,
#      even though the bodies are identical. The `dupes` line below records
#      that absence, so restoring extraction will fail this case loudly.
#
# Even once extraction is fixed, a define_method body has no `def` node and so
# no norm_hash under the current design; the block body is hashable structure
# and folding it is the follow-on question.
symbols app.rb  Alpha#one_step,Alpha#two_step,Widget#save
dupes           (none)
